{
  "version": 1,
  "title": "JsonUI include constraint targets — a constraint inside an include names its target as the target is spelled after expansion",
  "notes": "Ticket rjui-include-root-sibling-constraint-dropped, row D4. An include with an id prefixes the ids inside it (shared/core/include_ids_fixture.json, camelCase-joined as shared/core/camel_case_vectors.json); a relative constraint (align*OfView / align*View / alignCenter*View) between two elements of the same partial named its target by the unprefixed id, so the target was not found (sjui: constraint ignored; kjui: an unresolved ref that does not compile; the Dynamic runtimes: ignored). The rule: a target that names an element the partial declares itself (not inside a partial it includes, not an include node's id) takes the same prefix; any other target — and every attribute the include node itself carries, which lands on the partial's root — names the including layout's element and stays as written. `expected` maps each constrained element's expanded id to its constraint attributes and their expanded targets (elements without a constraint are not listed). Read by jsonui-cli (test_tools/tests/test_include_constraint_targets_on_every_platform.py: the sjui and kjui expanders) and, copied byte-identical, by SwiftJsonUI (Tests/SwiftJsonUITests/IncludeConstraintTargetsSharedFixtureTests.swift) and KotlinJsonUI (library-dynamic IncludeConstraintTargetsSharedFixtureTest.kt). The web (rjui) draws an include as a component and reads the same rule at run time: rjui_tools/spec/cli/include_root_sibling_constraint_spec.rb.",
